From 3775bdc9d3585ef5f5469d4a8ecc9384283e6139 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 00:12:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hir_expand: an async block or coroutine captures a variable's
 fields separately (edition 2021), as a closure does

localsend 0.1.0 (localsend-cli 1.18.2), compiling the core crate (-O):

    MIR ERROR: <localsend::webrtc::signaling::SignalingConnection>::start_listener BB17/2:
      ensure_valid: Use of non-valid lvalue - a0.0

webrtc/signaling.rs:282:

    pub fn start_listener(mut self) -> (ManagedSignalingConnection, mpsc::Receiver<..>) {
        ..
        tokio::spawn(async move {
            while let Some(message) = self.rx.recv().await { .. }
        });
        (ManagedSignalingConnection { client: self.client, tx: self.tx, on_answer }, rx)
    }

Since edition 2021 a closure captures the fields of a variable it uses
separately (RFC 2229), and rustc applies the same analysis to async blocks and
coroutines (they are closures to its upvar analysis).  mrustc's usage pass
already recorded field paths for closures but only whole variables for
coroutines, so the block above captured all of `self`; `self.client` then read
a moved value (a0.0 = the first field of the `self` argument).

The coroutine capture cache now holds the same `Capture { root_slot, fields,
usage }` entries as the closure cache:

- annotate_value_usage.cpp: a coroutine scope records the places used per
  variable, merged by the closure rule (a path below an existing capture folds
  into it, a path above replaces those below; the merge moved into a shared
  `merge_capture`).  `apply_coroutine` emits one capture per place; a `move`
  coroutine truncates a place reached through a reference to the reference,
  which moves in (as the closure code does, and as rustc does), and the
  shared-borrow upgrade and the propagation to an enclosing closure/coroutine
  use the place's type and path.
- closures.cpp: `coroutine_vars` gives a captured field path its own local
  and state field (typed by the path, the capture expression built as for
  closures); `ExprVisitor_GeneratorRewrite` rewrites a matching field chain in
  the body to that local, and a bare variable that was captured only by field
  paths is a BUG instead of an out_of_range.
- lifetime_infer.cpp: the capture-lifetime visit passes the path.

A by-value capture of a non-Copy place cannot move a field out of a type with
a Drop impl, so rustc truncates such a place to that type and captures it
whole (restrict_precision_for_drop_types; the common case is a `move`
block/closure that only borrows the field).  Closures never did this either:
`move || peek(&g.p)` with `g: Guard2` (Drop) moved `g.p` out of `g`.  The
truncation (and a re-merge of the shortened paths, also after the existing
deref truncation of move closures) now applies to closures and coroutines.

Editions before 2021 never record field paths, so their captures are the
whole-variable entries they were.

